# Every text file is stored with LF and checked out with LF, on every platform.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is not this repository's setting - it comes from the
# bundled PortableGit on the machine the tree was first cloned on - so without
# this file the same source is handed out as CRLF here and as LF on a Linux box,
# and whoever edits it on Windows commits a whole-file diff that says nothing
# about the change they made. An attribute travels with the clone; a
# `core.autocrlf` does not, and a clone made with `autocrlf=false` would keep
# the CRLF files CRLF and the LF files LF, which is the state this file exists to
# end.
#
# `text=auto` leaves the decision about what is text to git's own sniffing;
# `eol=lf` fixes what happens to the answer. Nothing in the tree needs CRLF:
# the `.ps1` scripts are already LF and run, and `xgview.rc` is already LF and
# `rc.exe` compiles it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, spelled out rather than left to the sniff. `text=auto` classifies by
# looking for a NUL byte in the first few kilobytes, which is right for all of
# these today - but a signing key or an icon put through a line-ending
# conversion is not a failure worth leaving to a heuristic, and the ones that are
# not in the tree yet are the ones nobody will remember to check.
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.ico      binary
*.icns     binary
*.webp     binary
*.ttf      binary
*.otf      binary
*.woff     binary
*.woff2    binary
*.so       binary
*.dylib    binary
*.dll      binary
*.exe      binary
*.apk      binary
*.aar      binary
*.jar      binary
*.jks      binary
*.keystore binary
*.zip      binary
*.gz       binary
*.mp4      binary
*.m4a      binary
